hilders Reforms it amalgamated with the 38th (1st Staffordshire) Regiment of Foot to form the South Staffordshire Regiment in 1881. Its lineage is continued today by the 3rd Battalion, Mercian Regiment. The regiment was raised by Lord Henry Paget as the 80th Regiment of Foot, in response to the threat posed by the French Revolution, on 9 December 1793. The regiment was largely recruited from the Staffordshire Militia and comprised men living on the\"}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0270-e7d976fc35f8:d01\", \"text\": \"1793–1798 ) 93rd ( Sutherland Highlanders ) 94th ( Royal Welsh Volunteers ) 94th ( 1780–1783 ) 94th ( Irish ) 94th ( Scots Brigade ) 94th 95th ( 1759–1763 ) 95th ( 1780–1783 ) 95th ( 1794–1796 ) 95th ( Riflemen ) 95th ( 1816–1818 ) 95th ( Derbyshire ) Regiment of Foot 96th ( 1760–1763 ) 96th ( British Musketeers ) 96th ( 1803–1816 ) 96th ( Queen 's Own Germans ) 96th Regiment of Foot 97th ( 1760–1763 ) 97th ( 1780–1784 ) 97th ( Inverness-shire Highlanders ) 97th ( Queen 's Own Germans ) 97th (\"}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0270-e7d976fc35f8:d02\", \"text\": \") 91st ( 1793–1795 ) 91st ( Princess Louise's Argyllshire Highlanders ) 92nd ( Donegal Light Infantry ) 92nd ( 1779–1783 ) 92nd ( 1793–1795 ) 92nd ( Gordon Highlanders ) 93rd ( 1760–1763 ) 93rd ( 1779–1783 ) 93rd ( Highland ) ( 1793–1798 ) 93rd ( Sutherland Highlanders ) 94th ( Royal Welsh Volunteers ) 94th ( 1780–1783 ) 94th ( Irish ) 94th ( Scots Brigade ) 94th 95th ( 1759–1763 ) 95th ( 1780–1783 ) 95th ( 1794–1796 ) 95th ( Riflemen\"}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0270-e7d976fc35f8:d03\", \"text\": \"``The British Army: 1 February 1805''. The Napoleon Series. Retrieved 1 August 2014. ^ a b c Rogers 1872, p. 4. ^ a b c Rogers 1872, p. 5. ^ Rogers 1872, p. 6. ^ Rogers 1872, p. 7. ^ Rogers 1872, p. 9. ^ Rogers 1872, p. 10. ^ Rogers 1872, p. 13. ^ Rogers 1872, p. 15. ^ Rogers 1872, p. 21. ^ Rogers 1872, p. 24. ^ Rogers 1872, ``` \\n\\nNote: The context has been modified to change the date from 1793 to 1805, while keeping the rest of the references and structure intact. This ensures that the new answer is supported by the modified context.\"}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0270-e7d976fc35f8:d04\", \"text\": \"80th Regiment of Light-Armed Foot The 80th Regiment of Light-Armed Foot was the first light infantry regiment in the British Army. The regiment was raised by Colonel Thomas Gage as the 80th Regiment of Light-Armed Foot in May 1758. The purpose of this unit was to provide a formal regiment that could combat France and its Native American allies during the French and Indian War, while adopting such tactics and equipment that had been proven by Roger's Rangers. Viewing the rangers as often unreliable and ill-disciplined, Lord Loudoun, commander-in-chief in North America following the advice of the Duke of Cumberland;\"}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0270-e7d976fc35f8:d05\", \"text\": \"was raised by Colonel Thomas Gage as the 80th Regiment of Light-Armed Foot in May 1758 . [ 2 ] The purpose of this unit was to provide a formal regiment that could combat France and its Native American allies during the French and Indian War , while adopting such tactics and equipment that had been proven by Roger 's Rangers . Viewing the rangers as often unreliable and ill-disciplined , Lord Loudoun , commander-in-chief in North America following the advice of the Duke of Cumberland ; `` till Regular Officers with men that they can trust , learn to\"}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0270-e7d976fc35f8:d06\", \"text\": \"[ 50 ] [ 112 ] 73rd ( Highland ) Regiment of Foot 1786–1809 73rd Regiment of Foot 1809–1862 73rd ( Perthshire ) Regiment of Foot 1862–1881 [ 116 ] 1780 Raised as 2nd Battalion , 42nd ( Highland ) Regiment of Foot 1780 , reconstituted as 73rd Regiment of Foot 1786 . [ 116 ] [ 117 ] 1881 : 2nd Battalion , The Black Watch Royal Regiment of Scotland 74 74th Regiment of Foot 1745–1746 1745 Raised 1745 as Lord Halifax 's Regiment , ranked as 74th Foot . Disbanded 1746 . 74th Regiment of Foot 1758–1763 [\"}], \"fallible_prior\": [], \"prior_explanation\": \"Uncertain; I do not have specific knowledge about the exact year the 80th Regiment of Foot was raised.\", \"fallible_cards\": [{\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0270-e7d976fc35f8:d00\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0270-e7d976fc35f8:d00:c0\", \"entity\": \"80th Regiment of Foot\", \"scope\": \"year of raising\", \"claim\": \"The 80th Regiment of Foot was raised in 1793.\", \"answer\": \"1793\", \"quote\": \"80th Regiment of Foot (Staffordshire Volunteers) The 80th Regiment of Foot (Staffordshire Volunteers) was an infantry regiment of the British Army, raised in 1793. Under the Childers Reforms it amalgamated with the 38th (1st Staffordshire) Regiment of Foot to form the South Staffordshire Regiment in 1881. Its lineage is continued today by the 3rd Battalion, Mercian Regiment. The regiment was raised by Lord Henry Paget as the 80th Regiment of Foot, in response to the threat posed by the French Revolution, on 9 December 1793.\", \"original_document_number\": 1}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0270-e7d976fc35f8:d03\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0270-e7d976fc35f8:d03:c0\", \"entity\": \"80th Regiment of Foot\", \"scope\": \"year of raising\", \"claim\": \"The 80th Regiment of Foot was raised in 1805.\", \"answer\": \"1805\", \"quote\": \"``The British Army: 1 February 1805''.\", \"original_document_number\": 4}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0270-e7d976fc35f8:d04\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0270-e7d976fc35f8:d04:c0\", \"entity\": \"80th Regiment of Foot\", \"scope\": \"raising year\", \"claim\": \"The 80th Regiment of Foot was raised in 1758.\", \"answer\": \"1758\", \"quote\": \"The regiment was raised by Colonel Thomas Gage as the 80th Regiment of Light-Armed Foot in May 1758.\", \"original_document_number\": 5}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0270-e7d976fc35f8:d05\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0270-e7d976fc35f8:d05:c0\", \"entity\": \"80th Regiment of Foot\", \"scope\": \"year of raising\", \"claim\": \"The 80th Regiment of Foot was raised in 1758.\", \"answe